// Plants that climb what they are given: ivy up a wall, a creeper over a house.
//
//     import 'climbers' { Ivy, Creeper }
//
//     cube { scale [3, 1.5, 0.2]  translate [0, 1.5, 0.2]  named 'wall' }
//
//     object Ivy('wall', 2.5) { translate [-1, 0.05, -0.03] }
//     object Creeper('wall', 2, 'autumn') { translate [1.5, 0.05, -0.03] }
//
// Each is a grown tree climbing the surface named all over (see `grown` in the documentation): points are
// spread over the whole of the surface, and the stems grow toward them from the place on it nearest where the
// plant stands, every step walked along the surface -- up a face, into a corner, over the top of a wall and down
// its far side.  So **the plant must stand at the foot of what it climbs**, within a few centimeters of it, and
// what it climbs must stand beside it in the same group, named.
//
// They take the name of what they climb, then the two things every plant in these libraries takes, in the same
// order and with the same meanings -- **what time of year** and **which one of that kind** -- after **how far it
// has spread**: the longest its stems run from its root, in meters.  A young plant covers a meter or two about
// its foot; an old one, the house.  Only the name is ever required.
//
// | `Ivy` | English ivy: evergreen, dark glossy lobed leaves held flat against the wall.  In winter a third bronzed. |
// | `Creeper` | Virginia creeper: five leaflets on long stalks, green in summer, crimson in autumn, bare in winter. |
//
// **The seasons are the same words** as the trees': 'summer', 'autumn' (or 'fall'), 'winter', or anything else
// for spring.  Ivy hardly changes; a creeper is what a wall in autumn is remembered for.
//
// **Everything is in meters**, as a leaf is the size a leaf is: the leaves and the stems are drawn at their own
// size whatever they climb, so a climber on a model's wall a unit high is a climber on a wall a meter high.
//
// -- What is here for the library and not for you --------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything beginning with `Climber` is the machinery.  The materials travel with what they belong to, for the
// reason the trees library gives, and are prefixed so they will not collide with anything of yours.

ClimberIvyStem = material { pigment [0.26, 0.22, 0.17]  specular 0.06  shininess 10 }
ClimberCreeperStem = material { pigment [0.30, 0.20, 0.14]  specular 0.06  shininess 10 }

// -- Ivy -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

